## Summary

The script half is strong and I verified it works, not just read it:

• --test → ALL PASSED, exit 0; --help → real argparse, exit 0 (the measure_abstentions.py trap avoided)
• `--census → real reading: **12 task commits, 5 NO_TEST_CHANGE, 7 addressable, 5 register-only, 2 behavioural (17%)** over 52 commits reachable from HEAD (52 total, shallow=yes)` — stated as a commit window, not a time window
• Positive control run serially on a /tmp copy: neutering has_compile_error reddens exactly the 3 INCONCLUSIVE/precedence rows (collapsing them to UNEARNED) while EARNED/NO_TEST_CHANGE stay green, exit 1. The discriminator is load-bearing and the classifier can fail.
• All three landmines handled: scratch git worktree add --detach under mkdtemp() with remove --force in a finally, its own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, and nothing wired into cargo test
• Every definition has an in-file consumer (checked by hand — Python has no dead-code gate, so the green build proves nothing here)
• The register-only split is more careful than the task asked for: it correctly keeps the flattering 58% out of the headline

Why it fails anyway: CLAUDE.md is entirely absent from the commit — single-file diff, clean tree, grep -c "counterfactual\|EARNED\|UNEARNED" CLAUDE.md → 0. The task listed it in Files and dedicated a whole required section to it, including the measured census number that the task itself calls "the deliverable ... and it decides the milestone."

That number is now only in a script's stdout. This repo's own documentation records, repeatedly and with receipts, that findings left in ephemeral surfaces evaporate while those routed to durable ones get acted on — the 17% behavioural rate is precisely the "small number is the finding, report it verbatim either way" result, and it survives nowhere. That's half the named deliverable missing, not a style nit, so doc_sync: FAIL forces the overall verdict.

The remedy is small and the work is otherwise sound: one bullet under the scripts family carrying the five states, the discriminator, the three landmines, the census number with its window, and the intent-vs-verdict limit.
